Sure, I'll provide you with the steps to create a simplified version where only the project name is captured:

1. **Generate a new Rails application**: If you haven't already, create a new Rails application using the `rails new` command.

2. **Set up Devise for authentication**: Install Devise and set it up according to your needs. Follow the instructions in the Devise documentation to add user authentication to your Rails application.

3. **Generate a Project model**: Use the Rails scaffold generator to create a Project model along with its corresponding controller, views, and routes. Run the following command in your terminal:

   ```bash
   rails generate scaffold Project name:string user:references
   ```

   This will generate the necessary files for your Project model, including the association with the User model.

4. **Run migrations**: After generating the scaffold, run migrations to create the projects table in the database:

   ```bash
   rails db:migrate
   ```

5. **Modify the Projects Controller**:
   - Update the `create` action in the `ProjectsController` to associate the newly created project with the current user.

   ```ruby
   # app/controllers/projects_controller.rb

   def create
     @project = current_user.projects.build(project_params)

     if @project.save
       redirect_to projects_path, notice: 'Project was successfully created.'
     else
       render :new
     end
   end
   ```

6. **Create a button for project creation**:
   - Add a button to the project manager page (`app/views/projects/index.html.erb`) for creating new projects. This button will open a form to create a new project.

   ```html
   <!-- app/views/projects/index.html.erb -->

   <h1>Project Manager</h1>

   <%= link_to "Create New Project", new_project_path, class: "btn btn-primary mb-3" %>

   <%= render @projects %>
   ```

7. **Create a form for creating projects**:
   - Create a new view file for the project creation form (`app/views/projects/new.html.erb`).

   ```html
   <!-- app/views/projects/new.html.erb -->

   <h2>Create New Project</h2>

   <%= form_with(model: @project, url: projects_path) do |form| %>
     <div>
       <%= form.label :name %>
       <%= form.text_field :name %>
     </div>
     <div>
       <%= form.submit "Create Project", class: "btn btn-primary" %>
     </div>
   <% end %>
   ```

8. **Modify the Projects Controller to handle the new action**:
   - Update the `new` action in the `ProjectsController` to instantiate a new project object.

   ```ruby
   # app/controllers/projects_controller.rb

   def new
     @project = Project.new
   end
   ```

9. **Ensure Authentication for Users**:
   - Make sure the project manager page is only accessible to authenticated users. You can use Devise's `authenticate_user!` method as a before_action in your ProjectsController.

   ```ruby
   # app/controllers/projects_controller.rb

   before_action :authenticate_user!
   ```

10. **Display Projects for Current User Only**:
    - Modify the index action in the `ProjectsController` to fetch only the projects belonging to the current user.

    ```ruby
    # app/controllers/projects_controller.rb

    def index
      @projects = current_user.projects
    end
    ```

11. **Update Views to display user's projects**:
    - Modify the views to only display projects belonging to the current user. You can iterate over `@projects` in the index view and display them accordingly.

    ```html
    <!-- app/views/projects/index.html.erb -->

    <h1>My Projects</h1>
    <ul>
      <% @projects.each do |project| %>
        <li><%= project.name %></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
    ```

With these steps, users will be able to create projects with only a name through a button on the project manager page, and each project will be associated with the user who created it. Users will only see their own projects on the project manager page.



